        "That does not mean good service automatically guarantees growth. Sales, positioning, pricing, market demand, delivery capacity, and cash flow still matter. But in a service business, poor delivery undermines retention and referrals. A strong service creates the conditions for repeat work and trust.\n2.1 The difference between a freelancer and an agency\nDimension\tFreelancer model\tAgency model\nMain delivery\tOne person's skill\tA coordinated set of capabilities\nTypical scope\tSpecific task or project\tA defined service system\nPricing pattern\tPer project, shoot, or deliverable\tOften monthly retainer or recurring engagement\nClient relationship\tMay be occasional\tDesigned for ongoing work\nCapacity\tLimited by one person's time\tExpanded through team and processes\nAccountability\tMainly for own work\tFor the agreed service and team delivery\nGrowth constraint\tPersonal output\tHiring, management, quality, and systems",
        "A freelancer can earn well and remain a freelancer by choice. An agency is not automatically better. It introduces more responsibility: payroll, coordination, client communication, quality control, and operational risk.\n2.2 Do not sell a list of disconnected tasks\nA weak offer sounds like:\n- We make videos.\n- We write captions.\n- We design posts.\n- We run ads.\n- We manage websites.\nA stronger offer explains how those services work together:\n- We plan and produce a brand's short-form content.\n- We manage the publishing calendar.\n- We write scripts and captions.\n- We test creative concepts.\n- We report on the performance of the content and recommend the next actions.\nThe service mix should make sense to the customer. More services are not automatically more valuable. Add a service only when it contributes to a coherent result and can be delivered to an acceptable standard."
