        "The practical lesson is to create evidence before expecting a stranger to trust you with a paid project.\n4.1 Build a practice environment\nIf you have no clients, use a project that allows you to practise:\n- A family business\n- A friend's business\n- A student club or college event\n- A local organisation\n- A self-initiated mock brief\n- A personal content channel\nBe transparent about whether the work was paid, voluntary, or self-initiated. Never present a mock project as a real client engagement.\n4.2 Practice deliberately\nDo not simply repeat the same task. Each practice project should improve a specific part of the skill.",
        "For video production:\n- Framing and composition\n- Lighting\n- Audio\n- Shot planning\n- Story structure\n- Editing rhythm\n- Colour and consistency\n- Export quality\n- Platform-specific formats\nFor web development:\n- User research\n- Information architecture\n- Responsive layout\n- Accessibility\n- Performance\n- Forms and integrations\n- Analytics\n- Deployment and maintenance\nFor performance marketing:\n- Customer and market research\n- Offer analysis\n- Creative strategy\n- Campaign structure\n- Tracking and measurement\n- Budget management\n- Experiment design\n- Reporting and optimisation\nFor copywriting:\n- Customer interviews and review analysis\n- Positioning\n- Headlines and hooks\n- Offer clarity\n- Landing-page structure\n- Email sequences\n- Editing and proofing\n- Claim substantiation\n4.3 Set a minimum proof standard\nBefore pitching a service, you should be able to:\n1. Explain what you do.\n2. Show at least a few examples.\n3. Describe the problem each example solves.\n4. Explain your process.\n5. Identify what you would improve next time.\n6. Deliver the service without making promises you cannot keep.\nYou do not need a huge portfolio. You need enough relevant evidence to make a credible first conversation possible."
