        "The source founder compiled two to three minutes of his strongest footage and sent it to potential hospitality clients. This is a useful model: show the work, make it easy to review, and tailor it to the recipient's industry.\n5.1 A simple portfolio structure\nYour portfolio can be a one-page website, a PDF, or a concise presentation. Include:\n- A short statement explaining the service\n- Three to six strong examples\n- A sentence explaining the objective of each example\n- Your role in the work\n- Any relevant, verifiable outcome\n- Your process and deliverables\n- Contact information\nIf you have no measured business outcomes, do not invent them. Explain the work itself and what you were trying to achieve.\n5.2 Show relevance, not just volume\nA restaurant owner is more likely to care about restaurant photography or short-form food content than a random montage of unrelated work. A SaaS founder may care more about a landing page, product demo, or conversion-focused design.",
        "Make the portfolio easy to scan:\n- Put the most relevant work first.\n- Keep the best examples.\n- Use clear captions.\n- Avoid long explanations.\n- Make the next step obvious.\n5.3 Build a case study from every successful project\nA useful case study answers:\n1. What was the client's situation?\n2. What did the client need?\n3. What work did you agree to deliver?\n4. What did you do?\n5. What was delivered?\n6. What happened afterward?\n7. What can you verify?\n8. What would you improve?\nDo not attribute all business growth to your service unless you can support that claim. Distinguish your contribution from other factors."
