        "He wanted to work with fewer clients but provide a wider set of services. This was the transition from being a person with a camera and laptop to building a coordinated service business.\n8.1 Identify the limits of the current offer\nAsk:\n- What does the client need before my work begins?\n- What does the client need after I deliver?\n- What work is the client still coordinating elsewhere?\n- Which recurring problems are not being solved?\n- What complementary services could improve the result?\n- Can I deliver those services reliably?\nFor the source founder, the initial services were filming and editing. The wider offer added content writing, scripting, ideation, social media management, website management, and performance marketing.\n8.2 Build around a customer workflow\nA coherent content and marketing service might look like:\n1. Strategy and planning\n2. Research and ideation\n3. Scripting and copywriting\n4. Production and design\n5. Editing and quality assurance\n6. Publishing and distribution\n7. Campaign management where agreed\n8. Measurement and reporting\n9. Next-month planning\nThe services should form a connected workflow. Do not add a website service simply because another agency offers it. Add it when the client needs it and your team can deliver it.\n8.3 Choose fewer clients only when the economics work\nThe founder wanted to move from many one-off clients to a smaller group receiving a fuller service. This can create deeper relationships and more predictable work, but a smaller client base also creates concentration risk.",
