        "A retainer can improve revenue predictability and help the agency plan capacity. It also creates a recurring obligation to deliver.\n9.1 What a retainer means\nA retainer is a recurring agreement under which the client pays a defined amount for an agreed scope or allocation of services over a period.",
        "A good retainer specifies:\n- Monthly fee\n- Service scope\n- Deliverables or capacity\n- Timeline and cadence\n- Client responsibilities\n- Approval process\n- Reporting\n- Meeting frequency\n- Revision policy\n- Out-of-scope rates\n- Contract term and cancellation terms\nA retainer is not a promise to do unlimited work.\n9.2 Example retainer packages\nThe following is an illustrative structure, not pricing from the source.\nPackage\tExample scope\tBest suited to\nStarter\tMonthly planning, a limited set of content assets, basic reporting\tBusinesses beginning consistent content\nGrowth\tPlanning, scripting, production, editing, publishing support, monthly review\tBusinesses needing a regular content operation\nIntegrated\tGrowth scope plus agreed campaign, landing-page, or website support\tBusinesses needing coordinated marketing execution",
        "Set pricing only after estimating the labour, tools, production expenses, management time, revisions, and expected margin.\n9.3 Retainer pricing formula\nStart with delivery cost, then add overhead and a target operating margin.",
        "Minimum sustainable fee = direct delivery cost + allocated overhead + risk buffer + target operating profit",
        "Direct delivery cost can include:\n- Team hours\n- Freelancers or contractors\n- Production expenses\n- Software used for the client\n- Travel or location expenses\n- Asset licensing\nAllocated overhead can include:\n- Management\n- Sales\n- Office and utilities\n- General software\n- Accounting and administration\nDo not price only by looking at a competitor's package. You need to know whether your own delivery model is profitable.\n9.4 Protect retainer quality\n- Set a clear monthly scope.\n- Maintain a delivery calendar.\n- Obtain approvals on time.\n- Report work completed and relevant results.\n- Track requests outside the agreement.\n- Review the relationship regularly.\n- Raise scope or pricing changes before work expands.\nThe goal is to create dependable value for the client and a sustainable delivery model for the agency."
