        "The underlying idea is to begin with a strong specialist capability and gradually assemble the complementary skills required to deliver a broader service.\n10.1 Start with your strongest capability\nYour core skill is the reason the first client may trust you. Keep improving it even after you begin managing people.\n10.2 Identify the capabilities around it\nFor a content and marketing agency, the capabilities might include:\n- Strategy and client understanding\n- Creative direction\n- Copywriting\n- Design\n- Video production\n- Editing\n- Performance marketing\n- Publishing and community management\n- Analytics and reporting\n- Account management\nNot every agency needs all of these in-house. The source founder preferred building the core services under one roof rather than outsourcing them, but that was his strategic choice. Other businesses may use specialist contractors or partners.\n10.3 Map your gaps honestly\nCapability\tCurrent level\tRequired level\tPlan\nCore service\tStrong\tExcellent\tContinue practising and lead quality\nCopywriting\tWeak\tReliable\tPartner, hire, or train\nPerformance marketing\tWeak\tReliable\tPartner, hire, or train\nGraphic design\tBasic\tReliable\tLearn or bring in a specialist\nClient management\tDeveloping\tStrong\tBuild a repeatable process\nOperations\tDeveloping\tStrong\tDocument delivery and ownership",
