        "This is a strong practical lesson: existing trust can make a new offer easier to sell than approaching an entirely cold market.\n12.1 Start with people who already know your work\nReview your existing contacts:\n- Previous clients\n- Businesses that referred you\n- People who have seen your work\n- Prospects who were interested but not ready\n- Businesses with recurring needs\nDo not assume they will buy. Ask whether the broader offer solves a problem they actually have.\n12.2 Explain the transition clearly\nA useful conversation structure:\n1. Acknowledge the work you have already done together.\n2. Explain what you learned about the client's needs.\n3. Identify the gap between the current service and the wider workflow.\n4. Present the proposed monthly scope.\n5. Explain how delivery and communication will work.\n6. Share the fee and terms.\n7. Invite questions and assess fit.\n12.3 Qualify before accepting\nNot every interested prospect is a good client. Check:\n- Is there a real business need?\n- Is the decision-maker involved?\n- Is the budget adequate?\n- Can the agency deliver the required work?\n- Are expectations realistic?\n- Is the client willing to provide timely inputs and approvals?\n- Is the payment process acceptable?\n- Does the engagement fit the team's capacity?\nChoose clients based on fit, not only the excitement of getting a yes."
