        "The lesson is to avoid treating an office as proof that a business is real. A service business can begin with a laptop, a reliable workflow, and appropriate meeting arrangements.\n13.1 Keep fixed costs low early\nBefore committing to rent, ask:\n- Is a physical office necessary for delivery?\n- Can the team work remotely?\n- Are there client confidentiality requirements?\n- Do production activities require a studio?\n- Can meeting rooms or coworking spaces solve the need?\n- Is revenue stable enough to cover rent?\n- Will the office improve output or merely increase overhead?\nThe coffee-shop approach in the source was a temporary workaround, not a universal recommendation. Respect venue policies, avoid occupying customer space inappropriately, and do not assume a café is a free office.\n13.2 Spend on bottlenecks, not appearances\nEarly spending priorities should usually be tied to delivery or sales:\n- Equipment required to do the work\n- Software required for production\n- Reliable internet and backup\n- Contractor or team capacity\n- Client acquisition\n- Accounting and legal basics\n- A workspace when operationally justified\nA polished office cannot compensate for an unclear offer or poor delivery.\n13.3 Expand location only when there is a business reason\nThe source mentions the agency eventually establishing a presence in another city. A second location can improve access to clients or talent, but it also adds management complexity and cost.",
